course: dictionary
lesson: 11070
title: Rivers and weirs
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A weir is a low wall or dam built across a river. It holds water back so the level rises behind it, and water pours over the top. «A weir is a low wall across a river that keeps the water deep upstream for boats.» The clue is keeps the water deep: the wall holds the water back.
explain: Look at the words around weir to check your meaning. «Rivers were tamed too: weirs keep the water deep enough for boats and drive water-mills.» Ask: what word would fit here? Dam fits, and so does wall, so a weir is a wall or dam across a river. Swap in your meaning and see if the sentence still makes sense.
explain: Some words warn you about danger. «The churning water below a weir is one of the most dangerous places to swim.» Churning means stirred up and swirling. When the context says dangerous, a weir is a place to keep well away from.
fact: A weir is a low wall or dam built across a river.
fact: A weir controls the flow of water and can raise the water level upstream.
fact: Water pours over the top of a weir.
fact: Fish ladders help salmon get past weirs.
fact: The water below a weir can churn and trap swimmers and boats, so never swim near one.
exercise: What is a weir? | A bridge for walking across a river | A small boat used for fishing | *A low wall or dam built across a river to control the flow of water | A fish that swims upstream
exercise: «Below the weir the water churns round and round.» What does churns suggest? | *Swirls and stirs, so it is dangerous | Stays perfectly still and calm | Sings a quiet tune | Freezes solid in winter
exercise: «Fish ladders help salmon get past weirs.» What do fish ladders help fish do? | *Get past the weir | Build a new weir | Stop swimming altogether | Turn into birds
test: «The water poured over the weir in a foamy sheet.» Where did the water go? | *Over the top of the weir | Back up the river to its source | Straight into the sea without a sound | Under the bridge into a pipe
test: «So never swim near a weir, because the current can trap you.» Why should you never swim near a weir? | Because weirs are too short to see | Because the fish will bite | *Because the water below can trap swimmers | Because boats are not allowed on rivers
exam: «Weirs keep the water deep enough for boats and drive water-mills.» Which meaning of weir fits best here? | A sharp rock at the bottom of the sea | A kind of boat with a sail | *A wall across a river that holds the water back | A bird that lives in reeds
